# Lecture 07B

## Probability of progeny

## Chapter 4

## Dr. Neil Voss

## October 20, 2026

=== layout: one-panel

# Learning objectives

@body

- Distinguish one birth order from all orders with the same counts.
- Use factorials and binomial coefficients to count arrangements.
- Calculate progeny probabilities for equal and unequal outcomes.

=== layout: one-panel

# *Probability in predictions of progeny distribution*

@body

- Genetic ratios result from\:
  - chance assortment of genes into gametes
  - chance combination of gametes into zygotes
- Probability calculations in genetics frequently use the addition and multiplication rules of probability together

=== layout: section

# Equally Likely Combinations

=== layout: one-panel


@body

- Assuming equal numbers of boys and girls.
- If a mating has already produced a girl, what is the probability that the next child will be a boy?
- A. 0, no chance
- B. &#188;, 25% chance
- C. &#189;, 50% chance
- D. &#190;, 75% chance
- E. 1, 100% chance
- F. slightly higher than 50%, like 53%

=== layout: one-panel


@body

- Assuming equal numbers of boys and girls.
- If a mating has already produced [*three girls*]{color=#A7074B underline=true}, what is the probability that the next child will be a boy?
- A. 0, no chance
- B. &#188;, 25% chance
- C. &#189;, 50% chance
- D. &#190;, 75% chance
- E. 1, 100% chance
- F. slightly higher than 50%, like 53%

=== layout: one-panel

# Does the family history matter?

@body

Assume each birth is independent, with P(boy) = P(girl) = 1/2.

- A family already has one girl. What is P(boy next)?
- A family already has two girls. What is P(boy next)?

*Explain your reasoning before calculating.*

=== layout: one-panel

# Each birth is a new trial

@body

- P(boy next) = 1/2 in both cases.
- Earlier births do not change the probability of the next birth.
- This follows from the model's independence assumption.

*A run of girls does not make a boy "due".*

=== layout: one-panel

# Problem: seven children

@body

Assume seven independent births, each with P(boy) = P(girl) = 1/2.

*What is the probability of four boys and three girls OR three boys and four girls?*

The birth order is not specified.

=== layout: one-panel

# One order is not every order

@body

Seven births; each has equal probability of a boy or girl.

- P(one specified order) = (1/2)^7^ = 1/128
- All boys: one order; all girls: one order
- Four boys and three girls: many different orders
- Count the orders before adding their probabilities.

=== layout: section

# Before we can do this problem we need to learn some math...

=== layout: section

# Factorials

=== layout: one-panel

# *Factorial*

@body

- For a positive integer n, n! multiplies the integers from 1 through n
  - factorial four, written as 4! is equal to\:
  - 4! = 1&#215;2&#215;3&#215;4 = 24.
- The number gets really big, really fast
- Short hand sometimes be used in the form\:
  - n! = n &#215; (n-1)!
  - 4! = 4 &#215; 3! = 4 &#215; 6 = 24

=== layout: one-panel

# *Factorial*

@body

- For a positive integer n, n! multiplies the integers from 1 through n
  - factorial four, written as 4! is equal to\:
  - 4! = 1&#215;2&#215;3&#215;4 = 24.
- [The number gets really big, really fast]{color=red}
- Short hand sometimes be used in the form\:
  - n! = n &#215; (n-1)!
  - 4! = 4 &#215; 3! = 4 &#215; 6 = 24

=== layout: one-panel

# *Numberphile 0!*

@body

- Why is 0 factorial equal to 1?
  - Why does 0! = 1
