=== layout: section

# Binomial Distribution

=== layout: one-panel

# *Binomial Distribution*

@body

- In statistics, the binomial distribution give the probability of the number of successes in a sequence of [*n*]{underline=true} independent [*yes/no experiments*]{underline=true}, each of which yields success with probability [*p*]{underline=true}.

=== layout: one-panel

# Binomial coefficient

@body

*C(n,k) = n! / (k! &times; (n-k)!)*

- Read C(n,k) as "n choose k".
- Number of ways to choose k positions among n trials
- Order within the chosen group does not matter.

=== layout: one-panel

# Factorials: a reference

@body

| n | n! | n | n! |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 0 | 1 | 8 | 40,320 |
| 1 | 1 | 9 | 362,880 |
| 2 | 2 | 10 | 3,628,800 |
| 3 | 6 | 11 | 39,916,800 |
| 4 | 24 | 12 | 479,001,600 |
| 5 | 120 | 13 | 6,227,020,800 |
| 6 | 720 | 14 | 87,178,291,200 |
| 7 | 5,040 | 15 | 1,307,674,368,000 |

=== layout: one-panel

# Evaluating binomial coefficients

@body

*C(n,k) = n! / (k! &times; (n-k)!)*

- C(8,6) = ?
- C(8,2) = ?
- C(7,0) = ?

Remember: 0! = 1.

=== layout: one-panel

# Evaluating binomial coefficients

@body

| Choose | Factorials | Cancel | Result |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| C(8,6) | 8! / (6! &times; 2!) | (8 &times; 7) / 2 | 28 |
| C(8,2) | 8! / (2! &times; 6!) | (8 &times; 7) / 2 | 28 |
| C(7,0) | 7! / (0! &times; 7!) | 1 / 1 | 1 |

=== layout: one-panel

# Pascal's triangle: n = 0 through 5

@body

| n | Coefficients from k = 0 through n |
| --- | --- |
| 0 | 1 |
| 1 | 1  1 |
| 2 | 1  2  1 |
| 3 | 1  3  3  1 |
| 4 | 1  4  6  4  1 |
| 5 | 1  5  10  10  5  1 |

@notes

Read across row n. The first entry is k = 0. Entries count arrangements, not probabilities.

=== layout: one-panel

# Pascal's triangle: n = 6 through 10

@body

| n | Coefficients from k = 0 through n |
| --- | --- |
| 6 | 1  6  15  20  15  6  1 |
| 7 | 1  7  21  35  35  21  7  1 |
| 8 | 1  8  28  56  70  56  28  8  1 |
| 9 | 1  9  36  84  126  126  84  36  9  1 |
| 10 | 1  10  45  120  210  252  210  120  45  10  1 |

@notes

Read across row n. The first entry is k = 0. Entries count arrangements, not probabilities.

=== layout: one-panel

# How many orders?

@body

Seven children, with four boys and three girls.

*How many ways can the four boy positions be chosen?*

- Choose k = 4 positions from n = 7.
- Write C(7,4) before calculating.

=== layout: one-panel

# Four boys among seven children

@body

- Choose four of the seven birth positions.

*C(7,4) = 7! / (4! &times; 3!)*

= (7 &times; 6 &times; 5) / (3 &times; 2 &times; 1) = *35 orders*

Each specified order has probability (1/2)^7^.

=== layout: one-panel

# Four boys among seven children

@body

- Choose four of the seven birth positions.

*C(7,4) = 7! / (4! &times; 3!)*

= (7 &times; 6 &times; 5) / (3 &times; 2 &times; 1) = *35 orders*

Each specified order has probability (1/2)^7^.

=== layout: one-panel

# Either four boys or four girls

@body

- Exactly four boys: 35/128
- Exactly four girls: 35/128
- These outcomes are mutually exclusive.

*P(either) = 70/128 = 35/64 &asymp; 54.7%*

=== layout: one-panel

# Pascal's triangle counts birth orders

@body

| Boys k | 0 | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6 | 7 |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| C(7,k) | 1 | 7 | 21 | 35 | 35 | 21 | 7 | 1 |

@notes

There are 128 equally likely orders in total. Four boys corresponds to 35.

=== layout: one-panel

# Seven children: worked answer

@body

- Four boys and three girls: C(7,4) = 35 orders
- Probability of each order: (1/2)^7^ = 1/128
- P(4 boys, 3 girls) = 35/128
- P(3 boys, 4 girls) = 35/128

*Either composition: 70/128 = 35/64 &asymp; 54.7%*

=== layout: one-panel

# Problem: six children

@body

Assume independent births and P(boy) = P(girl) = 1/2.

What fraction of six-child families have:

- No girls?
- Exactly one girl?
- Exactly two girls?
- Exactly three girls?
- Three or more boys?

=== layout: one-panel

# Pascal's triangle: n = 0 through 5

@body

| n | Coefficients from k = 0 through n |
| --- | --- |
| 0 | 1 |
| 1 | 1  1 |
| 2 | 1  2  1 |
| 3 | 1  3  3  1 |
| 4 | 1  4  6  4  1 |
| 5 | 1  5  10  10  5  1 |

@notes

Read across row n. The first entry is k = 0. Entries count arrangements, not probabilities.

=== layout: one-panel

# Pascal's triangle: n = 6 through 10

@body

| n | Coefficients from k = 0 through n |
| --- | --- |
| 6 | 1  6  15  20  15  6  1 |
| 7 | 1  7  21  35  35  21  7  1 |
| 8 | 1  8  28  56  70  56  28  8  1 |
| 9 | 1  9  36  84  126  126  84  36  9  1 |
| 10 | 1  10  45  120  210  252  210  120  45  10  1 |

@notes

Read across row n. The first entry is k = 0. Entries count arrangements, not probabilities.

=== layout: one-panel

# Six children: count the orders

@body

| k | 0 | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6 |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| C(n,k) | 1 | 6 | 15 | 20 | 15 | 6 | 1 |

@notes

For no, one, two, or three girls use columns k = 0, 1, 2, 3. For at least three boys, add those columns.

=== layout: one-panel

# Six children: answers

@body

| Question | Orders | Probability |
| --- | --- | --- |
| No girls | C(6,0) = 1 | 1/64 |
| Exactly one girl | C(6,1) = 6 | 6/64 |
| Exactly two girls | C(6,2) = 15 | 15/64 |
| Exactly three girls | C(6,3) = 20 | 20/64 |
| Three or more boys | 20 + 15 + 6 + 1 | 42/64 |

=== layout: one-panel

# Problem: eight kittens

@body

Assume independent sexes, with P(male) = P(female) = 1/2.

*What is the probability of even numbers of both males and females in a litter of eight?*

Count zero as even. Which possible numbers of females belong in the sum?

=== layout: one-panel

# Eight kittens: count the orders

@body

| k | 0 | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6 | 7 | 8 |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| C(n,k) | 1 | 8 | 28 | 56 | 70 | 56 | 28 | 8 | 1 |

@notes

Choose the even columns: k = 0, 2, 4, 6, 8.

=== layout: one-panel

# Eight kittens: the answer

@body

- Even female counts: 0, 2, 4, 6, 8
- Corresponding orders: 1, 28, 70, 28, 1

*P(even counts) = (1 + 28 + 70 + 28 + 1) / 2^8^*

= 128/256 = *1/2*

With eight total, an even female count also gives an even male count.

=== layout: one-panel

# Problem: six alleles

@body

A diploid species has six alleles of a gene.

- How many distinct genotypes are possible for an autosomal gene?
- How many sex-specific genotypes are possible for an X-linked gene?

Use the choose function. Separate homozygotes, heterozygotes, and hemizygotes.

=== layout: one-panel

# Six alleles: autosomal genotypes

@body

- Homozygotes: choose one allele, C(6,1) = 6
- Heterozygotes: choose two different alleles, C(6,2) = 15
- *Total: 6 + 15 = 21 genotypes*

=== layout: one-panel

# Six alleles: X-linked genotypes

@body

- Homozygotes: choose one allele, C(6,1) = 6
- Heterozygotes: choose two different alleles, C(6,2) = 15
- Females: 6 + 15 = 21 genotypes
- Males: 6 hemizygous genotypes
- *Total: 27 sex-specific genotypes*

=== layout: one-panel

# Six alleles: compare the totals

@body

| Gene location | Two-copy genotypes | One-copy genotypes | Total |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Autosomal | 6 homozygous + 15 heterozygous | Not applicable | 21 |
| X-linked | Females: 6 homozygous + 15 heterozygous | Males: 6 hemizygous | 27 |

=== layout: section

# Unequally Likely Combinations

=== layout: one-panel

# Problem: unequal probabilities

@body

Cross Aa &times; Aa and consider four independent offspring.

*What is the probability of exactly three dominant-phenotype offspring (A-) and one aa offspring?*

=== layout: one-panel

# Not all phenotype orders have equal weight

@body

- In Aa &times; Aa: P(A-) = 3/4 and P(aa) = 1/4
- Probability of one specified order with 3 A- and 1 aa:

*(3/4)^3^ &times; (1/4)*

Now count the positions where the aa offspring could occur.

=== layout: one-panel

# Binomial distribution: textbook notation

@body

*P = n! / (s! t!) &times; p^s^ &times; q^t^*

- s successes and t failures; n = s+t
- Success probability p; failure probability q = 1-p
- n! / (s! t!) counts arrangements.
- p^s^ q^t^ is the probability of each arrangement.
- Trials are independent, with the same p on every trial.

=== layout: one-panel

# Dr. Voss version: fewer letters

@body

*P(k of n) = C(n,k) &times; p^k^ &times; (1-p)^(n-k)^*

- n: total offspring
- k: offspring in the category of interest
- p: probability of that category
- Independent trials; same p on every trial

=== layout: one-panel

# Exactly three dominant offspring

@body

Aa &times; Aa; four offspring

- P(A-) = 3/4; P(aa) = 1/4
- C(4,3) = 4 birth orders

*P(3 A- and 1 aa) = 4 &times; (3/4)^3^ &times; (1/4)*

= 27/64 &asymp; 42.2%

=== layout: one-panel

# Quiz and exam setup

@body

*P(k of n) = C(n,k) &times; p^k^ &times; (1-p)^(n-k)^*

- No calculators on quizzes
- Identify n, k, and p from the question.
- Substitute correctly before calculating.
- Use Pascal's triangle or the supplied coefficient.

Example: C(4,3) &times; (3/4)^3^ &times; (1/4)^1^.

=== layout: one-panel

# Mendel's pea seed color

@body

- Yellow *seed* color is dominant to green.
- Cross two heterozygotes; consider six offspring.
- n = 6; k = number with yellow seeds; p = 3/4

*Find P(k) for k = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, and 6.*

*P(k of n) = C(n,k) &times; p^k^ &times; (1-p)^(n-k)^*

@notes

The legacy title said pod color. Yellow dominance applies to seed color; green pod color is dominant.

